]}>
  <p>No single account is “best” for every child. The useful question is what the money is for, who should control it, and which tax rules fit that goal.</p>
  <h2 id="account-overview">Account overview</h2>
  <ComparisonTable caption="Five child savings account types: purpose, control, and constraints" columns={['Account','Primary purpose','Control','Key constraint']} rows={rows} />
  <h2 id="start-with-goal">Start with the goal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Education is the primary goal:</strong> begin by studying 529 rules and your state’s plan incentives.</li>
    <li><strong>The child may receive the $1,000 pilot contribution:</strong> check auto-account and pilot-election status even if another account remains the main savings vehicle.</li>
    <li><strong>Maximum spending flexibility matters:</strong> learn the irrevocable ownership and state transfer-age rules of UGMA/UTMA accounts, then verify current financial-aid treatment from official sources.</li>
    <li><strong>The child has earned income:</strong> a Custodial Roth IRA may create a distinct retirement pathway.</li>
    <li><strong>Disability-related eligibility applies:</strong> ABLE rules deserve a dedicated comparison before using a general custodial account.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">This comparison is educational, not a recommendation. State tax treatment, financial-aid treatment, benefits eligibility, and family circumstances can change the result.</div>
  <h2 id="custodial-accounts">UGMA/UTMA: flexible use, permanent child ownership</h2>
  <p>A custodial gift belongs to the child, not the donor. A custodian manages it for the child’s benefit until the applicable state transfer age; the beneficiary cannot simply be replaced. Flexible spending is not a promise of tax-free growth or permanent parental control. Verify the governing state law and current tax and financial-aid rules before making an irrevocable gift. <a href="https://syndication.finra.org/content/saving-college-ugma-and-utma-custodial-accounts">FINRA’s custodial-account overview</a> explains the structure; its historical tax figures are not current limits.</p>
  <h2 id="custodial-roth-ira">Custodial Roth IRA: start with eligible compensation</h2>
  <p>A child’s regular IRA contribution is limited by eligible taxable compensation and the annual combined IRA limit; Roth income restrictions can also apply. A gift does not itself create compensation. Confirm compensation records, the custodian arrangement, and withdrawal rules rather than treating this as a general-purpose spending account. See the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-ira-contribution-limits">IRS contribution rules and examples</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="able-accounts">ABLE: compare disability-related needs separately</h2>
  <p>ABLE accounts are tax-advantaged programs for eligible people with disabilities. Qualified disability expenses—not general spending flexibility—drive the tax treatment. Check current eligibility, contribution rules, permitted expenses, and interaction with public benefits with the program and a qualified benefits adviser. The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/government-entities/federal-state-local-governments/able-accounts-tax-benefit-for-people-with-disabilities">IRS ABLE overview</a> describes the purpose; its historical examples should not be used as current-year limits.</p>
